    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. one thing im worried about is the 'edge cases' where things look fine to a sighted user but fall apart for someone using assistive tech. like, weird little glitches in the UI or elements that just don't behave right during navigation. how should we approach identifying and documenting those hidden issues or small bugs that tend to slip through during testing?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For edge cases related to accessibility, it's important to engage with a diverse group of users, including those who use assistive technologies. Participate in beta testing with a focus group that represents various disabilities to ensure you get direct feedback. Additionally, automated testing tools can screen for common issues, while manual exploratory testing can help uncover less obvious problems. Document all findings carefully, with details about the issue, conditions under which it occurs, and any provided solutions. Consider a continuous integration process with accessibility tools in your automated pipeline to catch these issues early in the development cycle. Lastly, maintaining an accessible bug tracker will help prioritize issues as they're reported.",
      "source": "target",
